import{c,$ as n,Q as a,j as i,m as d,h as p,ax as s,n as o,o as l,S as h,g as r}from"./chunks/framework.DGHXLY8X.js";const T=JSON.parse('{"title":"Architecture","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"development/architecture.md","filePath":"development/architecture.md","lastUpdated":1780023194000}'),m={name:"development/architecture.md"};function u(f,e,g,S,v,b){const t=n("Mermaid");return a(),i("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=d('<h1 id="architecture" tabindex="-1">Architecture <a class="header-anchor" href="#architecture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Architecture&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page explains why VideoNode is structured as a Go daemon that supervises native C++ subprocesses, rather than a monolithic binary. It does not walk through setup steps. See <a href="./building">building</a> for that, or <a href="./../reference/pipeline-model">pipeline model</a> for entity-level semantics.</p><h2 id="why-separate-native-binaries" tabindex="-1">Why separate native binaries? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-separate-native-binaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why separate native binaries?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>V4L2 capture, RGA color-space conversion, and EGL/GLES GPU compositing are CPU- and kernel-interface-heavy workloads that cannot run safely inside a Go process. Go&#39;s runtime scheduler conflicts with the real-time V4L2 buffer queue, and cgo boundaries at frame rate introduce latency spikes. Running the video work in separate C++ processes with dedicated OS threads eliminates both problems. The Go daemon manages policy and the HTTP API; the C++ binaries handle frames.</p><p>The three binaries are:</p><ul><li><code>videonode-source</code>: V4L2 capture and RGA NV12 conversion, one process per source ID. Broadcasts NV12 dma-bufs to up to 16 consumers over a Unix socket.</li><li><code>videonode-composer</code>: EGL/GLES GPU compositor on Mali-G610. Reads NV12 dma-bufs from one or more source sockets and produces a BGRA canvas frame.</li><li><code>videonode-sink</code>: single-stream NV12 carrier. Dials a source socket and writes raw NV12 bytes to stdout for piping into ffmpeg.</li></ul><h2 id="zero-copy-frame-passing-with-scm-rights" tabindex="-1">Zero-copy frame passing with SCM_RIGHTS <a class="header-anchor" href="#zero-copy-frame-passing-with-scm-rights" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Zero-copy frame passing with SCM_RIGHTS&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Copying video frames over pipes at 30–60 fps would saturate memory bandwidth. Instead, <code>videonode-source</code> passes dma-buf file descriptors to consumers using <code>SCM_RIGHTS</code> ancillary data on a Unix socket. The consumer mmaps the kernel buffer directly, with no copy and no intermediate allocation. A serialized <code>dmabuf_msg::Header</code> (NV12 plane offsets, pitches, frame index) precedes the file descriptors on the byte stream. This wire format is defined in <code>composer/src/scm_rights_producer.cpp</code> and <code>scm_rights_source.cpp</code>; it is not compatible with the GStreamer <code>unixfdsrc</code> wire format.</p><p>Because <code>SCM_RIGHTS</code> supports N receivers on one socket, a single <code>videonode-source</code> can simultaneously feed a <code>videonode-composer</code> and one or more <code>videonode-sink</code> instances without any frame duplication.</p><h2 id="per-instance-grpc-control-plane" tabindex="-1">Per-instance gRPC control plane <a class="header-anchor" href="#per-instance-grpc-control-plane" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Per-instance gRPC control plane&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The daemon needs to reconfigure running processes (swapping the V4L2 device, updating canvas dimensions, pushing layout changes) without restarting them. A daemon-wide message bus would serialize all control RPCs through one bottleneck; a per-instance Unix-domain socket keeps each binary&#39;s control path independent. The daemon dials each socket immediately after spawning the binary, calls <code>Describe()</code> to record PID and protocol version, then issues unary control RPCs as needed.</p><p>Sources expose: <code>SetFormat</code>, <code>SetDevice</code>, <code>GetStatus</code>, <code>StreamStatus</code> (server-streaming status push), <code>Snapshot</code>, <code>Shutdown</code>.</p><p>Composers expose: <code>SetCanvas</code>, <code>SetSource</code>, <code>ClearSource</code>, <code>SetLayout</code>, <code>SetEffects</code>, <code>SetSourceState</code>, <code>GetStats</code>, <code>Snapshot</code>, <code>Shutdown</code>.</p><p>The <code>StreamStatus</code> stream is the only long-lived RPC. The source pushes a <code>Status</code> frame on health change, on consumer-count change, and once per second as a heartbeat. The daemon&#39;s <code>pipelinectl.Manager</code> pumps these onto a buffered channel for the SSE fan-out path.</p><p>Wire schemas live in <code>proto/control/source.proto</code> and <code>proto/control/composer.proto</code>. Generated Go stubs are in <code>internal/streams/pipelinectl/pb/</code>.</p><h2 id="process-supervision-contract" tabindex="-1">Process supervision contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#process-supervision-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Process supervision contract&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>videonode-source</code> sets <code>PR_SET_PDEATHSIG</code> so it receives <code>SIGTERM</code> if the daemon exits without a clean shutdown, preventing orphan processes. Sinks and composers reconnect to their source socket with 100 ms backoff for up to 30 s if the producer restarts. A sink crash does not take the source down. The daemon&#39;s <code>pipelinectl.Manager</code> evicts a source from its registry after 30 s of unresponsive <code>StreamStatus</code> reconnects.</p><h2 id="relationship-diagram" tabindex="-1">Relationship diagram <a class="header-anchor" href="#relationship-diagram" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Relationship diagram&quot;">​</a></h2>',18)),(a(),p(h,null,{default:s(()=>[l(t,{id:"mermaid-69",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20TB%0A%20%20accTitle%3A%20VideoNode%20process%20architecture%0A%20%20accDescr%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20The%20Go%20daemon%20spawns%20videonode-source%20and%20videonode-composer%20via%20gRPC%0A%20%20%20%20control%20sockets.%20Sources%20broadcast%20NV12%20dma-bufs%20over%20SCM_RIGHTS%20to%0A%20%20%20%20videonode-sink%20and%20videonode-composer.%20Each%20sink%20pipes%20raw%20bytes%20into%0A%20%20%20%20ffmpeg%20for%20encoding%20and%20RTSP%2FSRT%2FWebRTC%20egress.%0A%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20subgraph%20daemon%5B%22videonode%20(Go%20daemon)%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20direction%20LR%0A%20%20%20%20api%5B%22HTTP%20API%22%5D%20--%3E%20mgr%5B%22pipelinectl.Manager%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20muxers%5B%22RTSP%20%2F%20SRT%20%2F%20WebRTC%20servers%22%5D%0A%20%20end%0A%0A%20%20mgr%20--%20%22gRPC%22%20--%3E%20src%5B%22videonode-source%22%5D%0A%20%20mgr%20--%20%22gRPC%22%20--%3E%20comp%5B%22videonode-composer%22%5D%0A%0A%20%20src%20--%20%22NV12%20(SCM_RIGHTS)%22%20--%3E%20comp%0A%20%20src%20--%20%22NV12%20(SCM_RIGHTS)%22%20--%3E%20sink%5B%22videonode-sink%22%5D%0A%20%20comp%20--%20%22BGRA%20(SCM_RIGHTS)%22%20--%3E%20sink%0A%0A%20%20sink%20--%3E%20ffmpeg%5B%22ffmpeg%22%5D%0A%20%20ffmpeg%20--%20%22H.264%2FH.265%22%20--%3E%20muxers%0A%20%20muxers%20--%3E%20viewers%5B%22viewers%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:s(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[2]||(e[2]=r("p",null,[o("For entity-level semantics (source, composer, stream lifecycles and CRUD), see "),r("a",{href:"./../reference/pipeline-model"},"pipeline model"),o(".")],-1))])}const k=c(m,[["render",u]]);export{T as __pageData,k as default};
